<!--
    A published frame address holds only the frame writes a reader of the
    published pointer can reach. MSVC's SEH prologue links a registration
    record into fs:[0] (a frame publication) and allocates a local below it
    with `push ecx`, whose garbage ECX is overwritten by `and [ebp-0x10],0`
    before the local's address goes to a call:

      0x401000  55 / 8b ec / 6a ff / 68 00 20 40 00     push ebp ; mov ebp,esp ; push -1 ; push handler
      0x40100a  64 a1 00 00 00 00 / 50                  mov eax,fs:[0] ; push eax
      0x401011  51                                      push ecx            ; the local at ebp-0x10
      0x401012  8d 45 f4 / 64 a3 00 00 00 00            lea eax,[ebp-0xc] ; mov fs:[0],eax
      0x40101b  83 65 f0 00                             and dword [ebp-0x10],0
      0x40101f  8d 45 f0 / 50 / e8 18 00 00 00          push &local ; call 0x401040
      0x401028  8b 45 f0 / 8b 4d f4                     mov eax,[ebp-0x10] ; mov ecx,[ebp-0xc]
      0x40102e  64 89 0d 00 00 00 00                    mov fs:[0],ecx
      0x401035  8b e5 / 5d / c3                         mov esp,ebp ; pop ebp ; ret
      0x401040  c2 04 00                                ret 4

    P6 dead-code holds (kuna_stackalias.rs stores_transparent): the
    publication holds every aliased frame byte, so the record's handler and
    state stores print. The `push ecx` store to ebp-0x10 lies wholly below the
    lowest published byte (ebp-0xc, the record), so its dead-store search
    steps over the fs:[0] link STORE and finds the full overwrite by the
    `and`: the store is dropped. Treating that STORE as an observer kept the
    store live, which made ECX an input:
    `unsigned int seh_push_ecx(unsigned int a0)` with `v2 = a0;`. Strict fix,
    one pass.

    mid_published publishes a pointer into the middle of a local array and
    reads below it through the reloaded global:

      0x401060  83 ec 10 / c7 04 24 01.. / .. 02 / .. 03 / .. 04   arr[0..3] = 1..4
      0x401084  8d 44 24 08 / a3 00 21 40 00                      g = &arr[2]
      0x40108d  8b 0d 00 21 40 00 / 8b 41 f8 / 03 41 fc / 03 01 / 03 41 04
                                                                  return g[-2]+g[-1]+g[0]+g[1]

    arr[0] and arr[1] lie below the published byte but a reader of g reaches
    them, so all four stores print (main printed none).
-->
